  "enrichment": {
    "lsass_pid_768": "Target PID 768 = lsass.exe (Local Security Authority Subsystem Service) - stores credential material in memory.",
    "granted_access_decode": {
      "0x1010": "PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION | PROCESS_VM_READ - the minimum rights needed to read another process's memory. Credential-dumping tooling that reads LSASS in place, rather than writing a dump file, requests exactly this mask.",
      "0x1000": "PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION only - no memory read. Normal for service-manager housekeeping.",
      "0x2000": "PROCESS_SET_LIMITED_INFORMATION - no memory read."
    },
    "call_trace_note": "The alerting CallTrace terminates in UNKNOWN(000002EE319439BF) rather than a named module. Sysmon writes UNKNOWN when the calling instruction pointer lies in committed memory that is not backed by any DLL on disk. That is the signature of reflectively-loaded code - a module mapped directly into the process by the implant, never written to disk. Benign LSASS readers (antivirus, LSM, WER) always resolve to signed, on-disk modules.",
    "source_process": "powershell.exe PID 1648 is the alerting process. Its parent chain and the -enc launcher are outside the captured window, but the same PID is the source of the periodic outbound connections below.",
    "beacon_note": "PID 1648 makes outbound TCP connections to 10.24.9.50:80 at a near-constant ~5.1 second interval throughout the capture. Fixed-interval callbacks with no user-driven variation are characteristic of command-and-control beaconing, not interactive browsing.",
    "account": "CORP\\j.marsh, a domain user account. The whoami.exe /user execution immediately before the LSASS read is consistent with an operator orienting themselves after gaining access.",
    "contrast_note": "svchost.exe also appears accessing lsass.exe in this window with masks 0x1000 and 0x2000. Neither grants PROCESS_VM_READ, and both resolve to signed on-disk modules; these are normal service-control operations, not part of the alert."
